# Day 17 (Week 3): Processes, Job Control and systemd Services

**Objective:** Manage running processes and understand how Linux services start, stop, and restart.

## Part 1: Processes and job control (morning)

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `ps aux` | list all running processes |
| `ps aux \| grep <name>` | find a specific process and its PID |
| `top` / `htop` | live process view (`q` to quit) |
| `kill <PID>` | ask a process to stop politely (SIGTERM) |
| `kill -9 <PID>` | force-kill a process (SIGKILL) |
| `killall <name>` | kill every process with that name |
| `sleep 300 &` | run a command in the background |
| `jobs` | list background jobs in this terminal |
| `Ctrl+Z` | pause the current foreground job |
| `bg` / `fg` | resume a job in the background / bring it to the foreground |

### Quick practice

```bash
sleep 300 &
jobs
ps aux | grep sleep
kill %1            # %1 is the job number shown by `jobs`
jobs
```

