## Part 2: What is a systemd service?

A service is a program managed by systemd. It can start at boot, restart automatically if it crashes, and send its output to the system journal.

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `sudo systemctl start <name>` | start now |
| `sudo systemctl stop <name>` | stop now |
| `sudo systemctl restart <name>` | stop, then start |
| `sudo systemctl enable <name>` | start automatically at boot |
| `sudo systemctl disable <name>` | do not start at boot |
| `systemctl status <name>` | current state plus recent log lines |
| `journalctl -u <name>` | full logs for the service |
| `sudo systemctl daemon-reload` | tell systemd to re-read unit files (run after every edit) |

Unit files live in `/etc/systemd/system/`. Sections:
- `[Unit]`: description and ordering
- `[Service]`: what to run and how
- `[Install]`: when to start it (`WantedBy=multi-user.target` means normal boot)

## Part 3 (afternoon): service for `backup.sh`

A skeleton is provided in `systemd/backup.service`. Fill in the TODOs, then install it:

```bash
sudo cp systemd/backup.service /etc/systemd/system/
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start backup.service
systemctl status backup.service
journalctl -u backup.service
sudo systemctl enable backup.service
```

Notes:
- `backup.sh` runs and exits, so `status` will show it as finished (`inactive (dead)`). With `Type=oneshot` that is normal.
- Check that a new `backup_<timestamp>` folder appeared in your backup location.
- If it fails, `journalctl -u backup.service` tells you why. The usual causes are relative paths, a missing `chmod +x`, or wrong permissions.

