## Part 4 (evening): kill and restart drill

`backup.sh` exits on its own, so use `heartbeat.sh`, a long-running script that writes a timestamp every 5 seconds. Skeleton service: `systemd/heartbeat.service`.

```bash
chmod +x scripts/heartbeat.sh
sudo cp systemd/heartbeat.service /etc/systemd/system/
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl start heartbeat.service
systemctl status heartbeat.service          # note the Main PID
tail -f /tmp/heartbeat.log                  # Ctrl+C to stop watching
```

Now the drill:

```bash
sudo kill -9 <Main PID>                     # simulate a crash
systemctl status heartbeat.service          # a NEW PID means systemd restarted it
sudo systemctl stop heartbeat.service       # a deliberate stop is NOT restarted
systemctl status heartbeat.service
sudo systemctl start heartbeat.service
sudo systemctl restart heartbeat.service
systemctl status heartbeat.service          # compare the PID again
```

**Record at each step:**

| Step | Command | Status | PID |
|---|---|---|---|
| Started | `start` | | |
| Killed | `kill -9` | | |
| Auto-restarted? | `status` | | |
| Stopped | `stop` | | |
| Restarted | `restart` | | |

